@page "/help"
@using JobHunter.Jobs

<PageTitle>Help</PageTitle>

<h1>Help</h1>

<p class="lead-line">How a job gets its score, its class and its flags.</p>

<section class="help-block">
    <h2>Score</h2>
    <p>The model scores seven areas from 0 to 2 each, for a total out of 14. When two levels could both fit, it takes the lower one.</p>
    <p>A Refresh only brings jobs in; it never calls the model. The Score button next to it sends the jobs waiting for a score, the ones added by hand first and then the newest, up to Max scores per run in Settings. Score again on a job page scores that one job. Without an API key, use Export, the score-jobs skill and Import on the Inbox instead.</p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="table table-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Area</th>
                    <th>2</th>
                    <th>1</th>
                    <th>0</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <th>Niche</th>
                    <td>AI developer tooling, agent harnesses, code-review agents, LLM or MCP infrastructure; or your configured stack keywords at the centre of a specialized product</td>
                    <td>Work next to your target niche, such as platform or backend engineering in a product company</td>
                    <td>Anything else, or the "AI" only means using an assistant such as Copilot</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Level</th>
                    <td>Staff, principal or lead scope</td>
                    <td>Senior, or level unstated with senior-shaped work</td>
                    <td>Junior, mid, graduate or intern</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Stack</th>
                    <td>Your configured stack keywords as the main language and runtime of the role's backend</td>
                    <td>Your stack keywords among several used, or serious backend work in another language</td>
                    <td>No backend or systems work</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Remote and timezone</th>
                    <td>Remote worldwide, Europe or EMEA</td>
                    <td>Adjacent timezone, US hours only, timezone not stated, or relocation offered</td>
                    <td>On-site with no relocation, or a region that rules out Europe and the US</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Contract form</th>
                    <td>B2B is normal or contractors are welcome</td>
                    <td>Not stated, or employment only</td>
                    <td>Employment through an entity or payroll you cannot use</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Compensation signal</th>
                    <td>At or above your target pay</td>
                    <td>Pay not stated, or between your minimum and target</td>
                    <td>Below your minimum pay</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Company signal</th>
                    <td>Named product company describing its own product and team</td>
                    <td>Named but generic, an outsourcing firm or consultancy hiring for its own teams, or an agency naming its client</td>
                    <td>Company not named, an agency withholding its client, or no role specifics</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p>The compensation signal is set by the app, not the model, whenever Settings holds a minimum or a target. The minimum is the B2B hourly rate over a year, or the employment minimum when the role is employment only.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-block">
    <h2>Class</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="table table-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Class</th>
                    <th>When</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th>A</th><td>Total 10 or more and no blocking unknowns</td></tr>
                <tr><th>B</th><td>Total 7 to 9, or 10 or more that misses a condition for A</td></tr>
                <tr><th>C</th><td>Total 4 to 6</td></tr>
                <tr><th>D</th><td>Total below 4, or removed by the filter rules</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p>Three caps hold a job at C whatever its total: pay below your minimum; a posting that requires United States work authorization you do not hold; and Niche and Stack adding up to less than 2. The Inbox shows classes A and B only.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-block">
    <h2>Blocking unknowns</h2>
    <p>Things the posting leaves open that keep a strong job at B instead of A. <code>timezone</code> is the exception: it is shown for information only and never holds a job back, since Remote and timezone already scores the hours.</p>
    <dl class="help-terms">
        <dt><code>b2b</code></dt>
        <dd>Whether a contract you can work under, per your Contract preference, is possible cannot be told.</dd>
        <dt><code>timezone</code></dt>
        <dd>The required hours cannot be told, or look to fall outside your accepted regions. Information only.</dd>
        <dt><code>us_authorization</code></dt>
        <dd>The role may require United States work authorization and the posting does not settle it.</dd>
        <dt><code>end_client</code></dt>
        <dd>The actual employer is not named.</dd>
    </dl>
</section>

<section class="help-block">
    <h2>Flags</h2>
    <p>Set by the app's own rules, not by the model. The amber and red ones count against a job, the green and blue ones speak for it. The Inbox shows the ones against a job, plus the stack-match and high-pay highlights.</p>
    <p>Stack match, home city and high pay follow a Settings save right away; H3, H4 and drops still depend on the remote policy read off the raw posting, so they only update on the next Refresh.</p>
    <dl class="help-terms">
        @foreach (JobFlag flag in Enum.GetValues<JobFlag>())
        {
            <dt><span class="chip @JobDisplay.FlagTone(flag, settings)"><code>@JobDisplay.FlagCode(flag, settings)</code></span></dt>
            <dd>@JobDisplay.FlagMeaning(flag, settings)</dd>
        }
    </dl>
</section>

<section class="help-block">
    <h2>Prefill browser</h2>
    <p>"Open and prefill" drives a separate Chromium that listens for remote debugging on a fixed port, with its own profile folder. Your normal browser cannot be used, because it is not started with remote debugging.</p>
    <p>Start it from a terminal:</p>
    <p><code>chrome --remote-debugging-port=9333 --user-data-dir=&lt;folder&gt; --no-first-run</code></p>
    <p>Point the app at it with the <code>Prefill:BrowserEndpoint</code> setting. Start it again by hand after a reboot or after it is closed; closing that Chromium window stops it. If it is not running, "Open and prefill" fails with a connection error such as "connect ECONNREFUSED …:9333".</p>
</section>
